etric and headroom_derived.json real.plankton, do not support that claim. The zero ceiling needs only a two-valued Yhat.\",\n        \"section\": \"Results (headroom check), claims_grounding.json claim on plankton\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"claim\": \"A practitioner who already uses PPI++ ... can obtain most of the predictable gain on a predictor whose optimal weight exceeds one by letting the weight leave [0,1]\",\n        \"evidence\": \"paper.tex Limitations (3) and the list of untested experiments (i); Table tab:final unclipped-linear column; analysis/headroom_derived.json oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp (nonzerols: cannot access 'paper_review_feedback*': No such file or directory\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_114006.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_114006.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw8qXWWGq9kDnyJsNmak","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YMWC7WkSpjrzFPLxU3Pf8e","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; grep -n -i -E \"binary|two-valued|practitioner|3 s|3\\\\\\\\,s|deadline|unclipped|clip\" paper.tex | cut -c1-400; grep -n -i -E \"binary|practitioner|deadline\" claims_grounding.json | cut -c1-300"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5766,"cache_read_input_tokens":40866,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5766},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afd4733c-34b6-4ce7-a568-97a495fa1372","uuid":"5e21eb7d-1b00-490c-9069-ff5bcbc85f62","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:24:46.787Z","request_id":"req_011Cgw8qWxWzG5UrCBNvBXBA"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YMWC7WkSpjrzFPLxU3Pf8e","type":"tool_result","content":"40:Prediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by t\n48:Our route is a nested one. PPI++ is a control-variate estimator whose control variate is $\\Yhat$ with a scalar weight clipped to $[0,1]$. We add a second control variate $g(\\Yhat)$, a recalibration of the prediction fitted on the labeled data by cross-fitting, and let a variance-minimizing weight vector combine the two. A selection rule falls back to PPI++ unless the recalibrated interval is at\n52:\\item \\textbf{Headroom analysis.} For control-variate mean intervals with fixed inputs we give the asymptotic variance in closed form (Section~\\ref{sec:theory}). Relative to PPI++ the gain depends on two quantities only: the clipping of the tuning weight, and the gap $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ between the correlation ratio of $Y$ on $\\Yhat$ and the squared Pearson correlation. We list the conditions under\n86:Here $f=0$ gives the labeled-only interval, $f=\\Yhat$ with $\\lambda=1$ gives PPI, and $f=\\Yhat$ with the estimated weight clipped to $[0,1]$ gives PPI++ (the clip is applied in the reference implementation we compare against; Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-source}). With an unclipped weight on $\\Yhat$ the explained share is $R_f^2=\\rho^2$, the squared Pearson correlation. If the feature set contains $m\n91:Two things can therefore shorten a PPI++ interval with fixed inputs: allowing the weight to leave $[0,1]$ when the unclipped optimum $\\lambda^\\star>1$, and capturing nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, measured by $\\eta^2-\\rho^2\\ge0$. The gain is \\emph{zero} when (i) $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, which includes every predictor taking only two values, since two points are always collinear\n107:PPI++ & $\\Yhat$ & scalar, estimated, clipped to $[0,1]$ & normal; primary comparator, also the fallback \\\\\n109:Recalibrated PPI (studied) & $\\Yhat$ and cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ & vector, estimated, unclipped & Student-$t$, $n{-}1{-}p$ d.f.; adopted only if $\\ge1\\%$ shorter than PPI++ \\\\\n120:\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction \n148:\\caption{Sealed final results by family: observed log width ratio against PPI++, derived percent reduction, oracle ceiling of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on the pool (derived), the part of the ceiling reachable by an unclipped linear weight, and the excess of observed over ceiling. Aggregate row: mean over families.}\n153:Family & Observed & Reduction & Ceiling & Unclipped-linear part & Observed $-$ ceiling \\\\\n168:We evaluated Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on each pool, using $\\rho^2$ and an isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$ on the full pool, $n=500$, $N=20000$, and the evaluator's PPI++ weight clip (for synthetic regimes, large-sample moments of the generator and the evaluator's episode plan). The population ceiling is not a bound on a single finite draw, and it excludes the estimation cost of $g$.\n171:For census income, the unclipped optimal weight computed from the pool is 1.34 (the clipped weight equals 1), and 0.0179 of the 0.0213 ceiling is reachable by an unclipped linear weight on $\\Yhat$ alone; the pool's $\\eta^2$ and $\\rho^2$ differ by only 0.0044. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.census_income]]\n172:So for this family the predicted gain is mostly a consequence of the clip on the PPI++ weight, not of nonlinear recalibration. For the synthetic family, the entire ceiling comes from the nonlinear-miscalibrated regime ($\\eta^2=0.71$ against $\\rho^2=0.48$), and the observed 0.0413 matches the ceiling 0.0416 within 0.0003. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n174:\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: both $Y$ and $\\Yhat$ are binary, $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is therefore exactly linear in $\\Yhat$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#rea\n178:\\emph{Regime-level headroom is much larger than the family-level average.} Within the nonlinear-miscalibrated synthetic regime alone, the same formula gives an oracle narrowing over an unclipped linear weight of 0.286 in log width for $(n,N)=(200,20000)$ and 0.178 for $(500,2000)$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rsens}), i.e.\\ roughly 25\\% to 16\\% shorter intervals; the gain falls as $r=n/N$ grows because the\n182:\\caption{Oracle recalibration gain over an unclipped linear weight on $\\hat Y$ (log width ratio, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling}) by sample-size cell, for the one synthetic regime with nonlinear calibration and for the census-income pool (derived).}\n216:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero where the predictor is already well calibrated and linear (census insurance, census education), a few percent where the PPI++ clip binds (census income) or where the c\n222:\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control \n224:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} A practitioner who already uses PPI++ and wants a shorter interval can obtain most of the predictable gain on a predictor whose optimal weight exceeds one by letting the weight leave $[0,1]$; additional recalibration pays only when $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is visibly nonlinear. Neither step was tested here against its coverage cost in isolation.\n227:With labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, a cross-fitted recalibration added as a second control variate to PPI++ shortened the mean interval by 2.2\\% on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% relative to PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. A closed-form headroom analysis explains why the gain is small\n240:Implementation statements in this paper refer to the readable files in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/}: \\texttt{frozen\\_winner\\_estimator.py} (the recalibrated interval), \\texttt{variant\\_label\\_free\\_basis\\_estimator.py} and \\texttt{variant\\_stacked\\_calibration\\_estimator.py} (the two contrast variants), \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py} and \\texttt{evaluate\\_search.py} (the two evaluators; the first h\n242:Settings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion \n247:For fixed $f,\\lambda$, the two terms of $\\hat\\theta_\\lambda$ are independent, so $V(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y-\\lambda^\\top f)/n+\\Var(\\lambda^\\top f)/N$. Expanding, $nV(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y)-2\\lambda^\\top c+\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda+r\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda$, a quadratic with minimizer $\\lambda^\\star=\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$ and minimum $\\Var(Y)-c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$. For $f=\\Yhat$, $c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c=\\Cov(\n283:\\caption{Pool-level moments used for the ceiling (isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$; synthetic rows are large-sample regime moments). $\\lambda^\\star$ is the unclipped optimal weight on $\\Yhat$ at $n=500$, $N=20000$, not applicable (---) to the synthetic regimes, which mix several $(n,N)$ cells.}\n293:plankton (binary $\\Yhat$) & 0.7004 & 0.7004 & 0.0000 & 0.841 \\\\\n75:   \"claim\": \"Plankton predictor and label are binary so the ceiling is zero while observed narrowing is 0.0359\",","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afd4733c-34b6-4ce7-a568-97a495fa1372","uuid":"f1558699-b42b-469f-beaf-49b787607c28","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:24:46.870Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"40:Prediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by t\n48:Our route is a nested one. PPI++ is a control-variate estimator whose control variate is $\\Yhat$ with a scalar weight clipped to $[0,1]$. We add a second control variate $g(\\Yhat)$, a recalibration of the prediction fitted on the labeled data by cross-fitting, and let a variance-minimizing weight vector combine the two. A selection rule falls back to PPI++ unless the recalibrated interval is at\n52:\\item \\textbf{Headroom analysis.} For control-variate mean intervals with fixed 