ne, the same formula gives an oracle narrowing over an unclipped linear weight of 0.286 in log width for $(n,N)=(200,20000)$ and 0.178 for $(500,2000)$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rsens}), i.e.\\ roughly 25\\% to 16\\% shorter intervals; the gain falls as $r=n/N$ grows because the\n182:\\caption{Oracle recalibration gain over an unclipped linear weight on $\\hat Y$ (log width ratio, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling}) by sample-size cell, for the one synthetic regime with nonlinear calibration and for the census-income pool (derived).}\n216:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero where the predictor is already well calibrated and linear (census insurance, census education), a few percent where the PPI++ clip binds (census income) or where the c\n222:\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control \n224:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} A practitioner who already uses PPI++ and wants a shorter interval can obtain most of the predictable gain on a predictor whose optimal weight exceeds one by letting the weight leave $[0,1]$; additional recalibration pays only when $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is visibly nonlinear. Neither step was tested here against its coverage cost in isolation.\n227:With labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, a cross-fitted recalibration added as a second control variate to PPI++ shortened the mean interval by 2.2\\% on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% relative to PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. A closed-form headroom analysis explains why the gain is small\n240:Implementation statements in this paper refer to the readable files in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/}: \\texttt{frozen\\_winner\\_estimator.py} (the recalibrated interval), \\texttt{variant\\_label\\_free\\_basis\\_estimator.py} and \\texttt{variant\\_stacked\\_calibration\\_estimator.py} (the two contrast variants), \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py} and \\texttt{evaluate\\_search.py} (the two evaluators; the first h\n242:Settings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion \n247:For fixed $f,\\lambda$, the two terms of $\\hat\\theta_\\lambda$ are independent, so $V(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y-\\lambda^\\top f)/n+\\Var(\\lambda^\\top f)/N$. Expanding, $nV(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y)-2\\lambda^\\top c+\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda+r\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda$, a quadratic with minimizer $\\lambda^\\star=\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$ and minimum $\\Var(Y)-c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$. For $f=\\Yhat$, $c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c=\\Cov(\n283:\\caption{Pool-level moments used for the ceiling (isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$; synthetic rows are large-sample regime moments). $\\lambda^\\star$ is the unclipped optimal weight on $\\Yhat$ at $n=500$, $N=20000$, not applicable (---) to the synthetic regimes, which mix several $(n,N)$ cells.}\n293:plankton (binary $\\Yhat$) & 0.7004 & 0.7004 & 0.0000 & 0.841 \\\\\n75:   \"claim\": \"Plankton predictor and label are binary so the ceiling is zero while observed narrowing is 0.0359\",","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw8qkizfSsq7MtKzhNgX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012nUpZjgRmRdqNZuFZoBHZ4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; for l in 40 52 91 120 174 216 224 227 242; do echo \"== $l\"; sed -n \"${l}p\" paper.tex; done; grep -n -i \"plankton\\|zero\\|deadline\\|3 s\\|3\\\\\\\\,s\" paper.tex | grep -v \"^\\(40\\|52\\|91\\|120\\|174\\|216\\|224\\|227\\|242\\):\" | cut -c1-300"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3792,"cache_read_input_tokens":46632,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3792},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afd4733c-34b6-4ce7-a568-97a495fa1372","uuid":"6b445ed8-43ad-4aa9-91b5-08f171d22c2e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:24:49.808Z","request_id":"req_011Cgw8qkEEFQe1cUFBGGkFY"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012nUpZjgRmRdqNZuFZoBHZ4","type":"tool_result","content":"== 40\nPrediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by the gap between the squared correlation of $Y$ with $\\Yhat$ and the correlation ratio of $Y$ given $\\Yhat$, plus a clipping effect on the tuning weight. We evaluate a cross-fitted recalibration (isotonic or binned) added as a second control variate, with a fallback to PPI++, on a sealed evaluation of four real pools and known-truth synthetic regimes. The interval is 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ (geometric mean over five families), 6.8\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and 30\\% narrower than the labeled-only interval; per-family reductions range from 0.2\\% to 4.0\\%. Pooled coverage is 0.934 (lowest family 0.91), below the nominal 0.95 although not rejected by the evaluator's exact tests, and the observed narrowing exceeds our population-level ceiling in four of five families. The evidence supports a small, regime-dependent gain; it does not show that the gain is free of a coverage cost.\n== 52\n\\item \\textbf{Headroom analysis.} For control-variate mean intervals with fixed inputs we give the asymptotic variance in closed form (Section~\\ref{sec:theory}). Relative to PPI++ the gain depends on two quantities only: the clipping of the tuning weight, and the gap $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ between the correlation ratio of $Y$ on $\\Yhat$ and the squared Pearson correlation. We list the conditions under which the gain is zero, including any binary-valued predictor.\n== 91\nTwo things can therefore shorten a PPI++ interval with fixed inputs: allowing the weight to leave $[0,1]$ when the unclipped optimum $\\lambda^\\star>1$, and capturing nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, measured by $\\eta^2-\\rho^2\\ge0$. The gain is \\emph{zero} when (i) $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, which includes every predictor taking only two values, since two points are always collinear; (ii) $\\Yhat$ is uninformative ($\\rho=\\eta=0$); or (iii) $n$ is small enough that estimating $g$ costs more variance than it removes, a loss of order $p/n$ in relative variance. These are asymptotic statements for fixed $g$; with a cross-fitted $\\hat g$ they require that $\\hat g$ converge to $m$ at an adequate rate, which we assume and do not prove here (derivation in Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-deriv}).\n== 120\n\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction are both binary. A fifth family is synthetic with four regimes (linear calibrated, nonlinear miscalibrated with a cubic predictor, uninformative, heteroscedastic), $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ and $N\\in\\{2000,20000\\}$. Each family has 100 independent draws; real-pool draws are taken with replacement from the pool, so the truth is the pool mean, and synthetic truths are analytic. Controls with a constant predictor, a permuted predictor and a shifted outcome are run separately.\n== 174\n\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: both $Y$ and $\\Yhat$ are binary, $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is therefore exactly linear in $\\Yhat$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.plankton]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton]]\n== 216\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero where the predictor is already well calibrated and linear (census insurance, census education), a few percent where the PPI++ clip binds (census income) or where the calibration is nonlinear (synthetic regime). The population analysis predicts the sign and the rough size of these margins for census income and synthetic data, and predicts none for binary-valued predictors.\n== 224\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} A practitioner who already uses PPI++ and wants a shorter interval can obtain most of the predictable gain on a predictor whose optimal weight exceeds one by letting the weight leave $[0,1]$; additional recalibration pays only when $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is visibly nonlinear. Neither step was tested here against its coverage cost in isolation.\n== 227\nWith labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, a cross-fitted recalibration added as a second control variate to PPI++ shortened the mean interval by 2.2\\% on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% relative to PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. A closed-form headroom analysis explains why the gain is small and regime-dependent: it is bounded by the weight clip and by the nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, and is zero for binary-valued predictors. It also shows that the observed gain is larger than that ceiling in four of five families, and coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n== 242\nSettings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion with degrees of freedom floored at 3; PPI++ weight clipped to $[0,1]$; fallback margin 0.99. The reference calls have a 3\\,s per-estimate deadline.\n128:The sealed evaluation completed with all controls passed, zero failed episodes, and the validity gate and the bootstrap criterion satisfied (final outcome \\emph{qualified}). Pooled empirical coverage over the 500 evaluated draws is 0.934 (467 covered) and the lowest family coverage is 0.91 (91 o\n136:Most of the gain over labeled-only inference is therefore already captured by PPI machinery; recalibration adds a small increment on top. Per family, the narrowing against PPI++ is 0.0287 for census income, 0.0018 for census insurance, 0.0023 for census education, 0.0359 for plankton and 0.0413 \n137:No family is wider than PPI++ on this measure, but the gain is near zero for two of five families.\n158:plankton & 0.0359 & 3.5\\% & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.0359 \\\\\n176:Asymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, \n218:\\paragraph{What it does not support.} The evidence does not show that the narrowing is free: coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below 0.95, and the baselines' coverage is unknown. The observed gain exceeds the ceiling in four of five families, so a part of \n277:Search pools: galaxy morphology (Galaxy Zoo-type crowd labels, \\citealt{Willett2013GalaxyZ2}; $n=300$, $N=5000$), Amazon deforestation ($n=100$, $N=1000$), gene expression ($n=500$, $N=10000$), protein disorder ($n=300$, $N=5000$), and synthetic regimes, 60 draws per family, reset seeds 1001--10\n293:plankton (binary $\\Yhat$) & 0.7004 & 0.7004 & 0.0000 & 0.841 \\\\","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afd4733c-34b6-4ce7-a568-97a495fa1372","uuid":"1d51ceba-948a-49f5-ba07-311bfb8b1341","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:24:49.869Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"== 40\nPrediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by the gap between the squared correlation of $Y$ with $\\Yhat$ and the correlation ratio of $Y$ given $\\Yhat$, plus a clipping effect on the tuning weight. We evaluate a cross-fitted recalibration (isotonic or binned) added as a second control variate, with a fallback to PPI++, on a sealed evaluation of four real pools and known-truth synthetic regimes. The interval is 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ (geometric mean over five families), 6.8\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and 30\\% narrower than the labeled-only interval; per-family reductions range from 0.2\\% to 4.0\\%. Pooled coverage is 0.934 (lowest family 0.91), below the nominal 0.95 although not rejected by the evaluator's exact tests, and the observed narrowing exceeds our population-level ceiling in four of five families. The evidence supports a small, regime-dependent gain; it does not show that the gain is free of a coverage cost.\n== 52\n\\item \\textbf{Headroom analysis.} For control-variate mean intervals with fixed inputs we give the asymptotic variance in closed form (Section~\\ref{sec:theory}). Relative to PPI++ the gain depends on two quantities only: the clipping of the tuning weight, and the gap $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ between the correlation ratio of $Y$ on $\\Yhat$ and the squared Pearson correlation. We list the conditions under which the gain is zero, including any binary-valued predictor.\n== 91\nTwo things can therefore shorten a PPI++ interval with fixed inputs: allowing the weight to leave $[0,1]$ when the unclipped optimum $\\lambda^\\star>1$, and capturing nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, measured by $\\eta^2-\\rho^2\\ge0$. The gain is \\emph{zero} when (i) $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, which includes every predictor taking only two values, since two points are always collinear; (ii) $\\Yhat$ is uninformative ($\\rho=\\eta=0$); or (iii) $n$ is small enough that estimating $g$ costs more variance than it removes, a loss of order $p/n$ in relative variance. These are asymptotic statements for fixed $g$; with a cross-fitted $\\hat g$ they require that $\\hat g$ converge to $m$ at an adequate rate, which we assume and do not prove here (derivation in Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-deriv}).\n== 120\n\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction are both binary. A fifth family is synthetic with four regimes (linear calibrated, nonlinear miscalibrated with a cubic predictor, uninformative, heteroscedastic), $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ and $N\\in\\{2000,20000\\}$. Each family has 100 independent draws; real-pool draws are taken with replacement from the pool, so the truth is the pool mean, and synthetic truths are analytic. Controls with a constant predictor, a permuted predictor and a shifted outcome are run separately.\n== 174\n\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: both $Y$ and $\\Yhat$ are binary, $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is therefore exactly linear in $\\Yhat$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.plankton]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton]]\n== 216\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero where the predictor is already well calibrated and linear (census insurance, census education), a few percent where the PPI++ clip binds (census income) or where the calibration is nonlinear (synthetic regime). The population analysis predicts the sign and the rough size of these margins for census income and synthetic data, and predicts none for binary-valued predictors.\n== 224\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} A practitioner who already uses PPI++ and wants a shorter interval can obtain most of the predictable gain on a predictor whose optimal weight exceeds one by letting the weight leave $[0,1]$; additional recalibration pays only when $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is visibly nonlinear. Neither step was tested here against its coverage cost in isolation.\n== 227\nWith labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, a cross-fitted recalibration added as a second control variate to PPI++ shortened the mean interval by 2.2\\% on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% relative to PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. A closed-form headroom analysis explains why the gain is small and regime-dependent: it is bounded by the weight clip and by the nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, and is zero for binary-valued predictors. It also shows that the observed gain is larger than that ceiling in four of five families, and coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n== 242\nSettings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion with degrees of freedom floored at 3; PPI++ weight clipped to $[0,1]$; fallback margin 0.99. The reference calls have a 3\\,s per-estimate deadline.\n128:The sealed evaluation completed with all controls passed, zero failed episodes, and the validity gate and the bootstrap criterion satisfied (final outcome \\emph{qualified}). Pooled empirical coverage over the 500 evaluated draws is 0.934 (467 covered) and the lowest family coverage is 0.91 (91 o\n136:Most of the gain over labeled-only inference is therefore already captured by PPI machinery; recalibration adds a small increment on top. Per family, the narrowing against PPI++ is 0.0287 for census income, 0.0018 for census insurance, 0.0023 for census education, 0.0359 for plankton and 0.0413 \n137:No family is wider than PPI++ on this measure, but the gain is near zero for two of five families.\n158:plankton & 0.0359 & 3.5\\% & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.0359 \\\\\n176:Asymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, \n218:\\paragraph{What it does not support.} The evidence does not show that the narrowing is free: coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below 0.95, and the baselines' coverage is unknown. The observed gain exceeds the ceiling in four of five families, so a part of \n277:Search pools: galaxy morphology (Galaxy Zoo-type crowd labels, \\citealt{Willett2013GalaxyZ2}; $n=300$, $N=5000$), Amazon deforestation ($n=100$, $N=1000$), gene expression ($n=500$, $N=10000$), protein disorder ($n=300$, $N=5000$), and synthetic regimes, 60 draws per family, reset seeds 1001--10\n293:plankton (binary $\\Yhat$) & 0.7004 & 0.7004 & 0.0000 & 0.841 \\\\","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
